1
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:04,870
Good evening.

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:08,570
I'm your guest lecturer, Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

3
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,510
I was expecting applause,

4
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:20,420
but I suppose stunned silence is equally appropriate.

5
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:23,280
I agreed to speak to you this evening,

6
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:26,720
because I was told that you're the best and the brightest

7
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:29,860
of this university's doctoral candidates.

8
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,160
Hmm. Of course, that's like saying

9
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,780
you are the most important electron in a hydrogen atom.

10
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:39,270
'Cause, you see,

11
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:43,410
there's only one electron in a hydrogen atom.

12
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:48,420
Best and brightest, my sweet patootie.

13
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:50,030
All right, let's begin.

14
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:52,300
Show of hands, who here is familiar

15
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:55,490
with the concept of topological insulators?

16
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:58,000
Don't kid yourselves.

17
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,720
I found another tweet from a student at Sheldon's lecture.

18
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:05,230
"Dr. Cooper has taken a relatively boring subject

19
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:08,220
and managed to make it completely insufferable.

20
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:11,980
Plus, he looks like a giant insect."

21
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:14,300
Look. Listen to this one.

22
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,560
"Does Einstein's theory explain why time flies

23
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:18,910
when you're having fun,

24
00:01:18,910 --> 00:01:20,800
but when you're listening to Dr. Cooper,

25
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,840
it falls out of the sky, dead?"

26
00:01:23,670 --> 00:01:24,890
Ooh, somebody took pictures

27
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:27,000
and uploaded them to their Flickr account.

28
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:29,420
Wow.

29
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:30,920
How do you get an entire lecture hall

30
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:33,100
to flip you off at the same time?

31
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,070
Apparently, if you're Sheldon,

32
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:37,060
all you need to do is turn your back.

33
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:40,100
Hey, Leonard, is your Wi-Fi down?

34
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,020
I can't get on.

35
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:42,600
Oh, Sheldon changed the password.

36
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,110
It's now "Penny is a freeloader."

37
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:47,590
No spaces.

38
00:01:47,590 --> 00:01:49,410
Thanks. What are you guys doing?

39
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:51,080
Sheldon gave a lecture at the university tonight.

40
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:52,240
We're reading the reviews.

41
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:53,850
Oh. How'd he do?

42
00:01:53,850 --> 00:01:55,760
Well, picture the Hindenburg meets Chernobyl

43
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,600
meets Three Mile Island meets Tron 2.

44
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:00,720
That bad, huh?

45
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,440
Read this woman's tweet.

46
00:02:04,230 --> 00:02:05,440
"Listening to Dr. Cooper

47
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:07,690
has made me want to start cutting myself again""

48
00:02:07,690 --> 00:02:09,180
Yeah.

49
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:14,320
Good evening, Leonard, Howard, Raj,

50
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:15,660
freeloader.

51
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:18,960
So, how'd the lecture go?

52
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:21,690
In a word, triumphant.

53
00:02:22,890 --> 00:02:24,760
Really? Triumphant?

54
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:27,120
Oh, yes, you should have seen those young people.

55
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,700
Thirsty for knowledge, drinking in my wisdom.

56
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,070
I may have changed a few lives today.

57
00:02:34,470 --> 00:02:36,790
Oh, please let me tell him.

58
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:39,680
I don't know, I kind of promised Howard.

59
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:41,120
Tell me what?

60
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,830
Actually, we should all share the moment.

61
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:44,370
Raj, if you would.

62
00:02:46,910 --> 00:02:49,900
Oh, tweets about my lecture.

63
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:57,210
That's rather unfair.

64
00:02:58,130 --> 00:03:00,070
That's downright cruel.

65
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:02,260
Plus, insects have six legs.

66
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:06,570
Yeah, I'm not familiar with the acronym "KMN""

67
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:10,040
From the context, we think it means "kill me now."

68
00:03:13,270 --> 00:03:17,350
Well, I suppose everyone's entitled to their own opinion.

69
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:19,480
I think I'll turn in.

70
00:03:21,010 --> 00:03:23,560
I didn't want to teach those poopy heads, anyway.

71
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:29,170
FYI, I think that's what Darth Vader said

72
00:03:29,170 --> 00:03:32,170
just before he started building the Death Star.

73
00:03:58,570 --> 00:03:59,880
Sheldon still moping?

74
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:01,550
Yeah, it's weird. Even though he didn't want

75
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:02,900
to give the lecture in the first place,

76
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:05,350
being rejected by those students really hit him hard.

77
00:04:05,350 --> 00:04:06,760
I know the feeling.

78
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:08,510
It's like accidentally walking into a gay bar

79
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:10,510
and then having no one hit on you.

80
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:20,680
It-It happened to a friend of mine.

81
00:04:22,670 --> 00:04:24,000
Are you guys doing okay?

82
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,320
Leonard and I are fine,

83
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:28,380
but I think Raj needs to meet a girl really soon.

84
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:30,540
Well, that shouldn't be too hard.

85
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:31,980
He's such a cutie.

86
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:33,730
Thank you, but "cute" is for bunnies.

87
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:35,520
I want to be something with sex appeal,

88
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:38,370
like... like a labradoodle.

89
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,620
Labradoodle?

90
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,710
We might be starting to zero in on your problem.

91
00:04:46,130 --> 00:04:47,170
Don't you listen to them.

92
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:49,030
You've got plenty of sex appeal.

93
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:50,040
You really think so?

94
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:52,080
Yeah. You're a hottie.

95
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:53,590
Well, thanks, Bernadette.

96
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,010
And just for the record,

97
00:04:57,010 --> 00:04:58,500
labradoodles are hypo-allergenic,

98
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,440
which is a very sexy quality to those troubled by animal dander.

99
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:07,590
So, what do you think?

100
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:13,400
It's a charming illusion, but it does not cheer me up.

101
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:15,260
Not even when I do this?

102
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:23,480
No.

103
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:27,270
Well, that was the last arrow in my quiver of whimsy.

104
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,500
Do you realize that teaching is the first thing

105
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:32,670
I've failed at since my ill-fated attempt

106
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:35,770
to complete a chin-up in March of 1989?

107
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:37,790
If this humiliating experience

108
00:05:37,790 --> 00:05:38,970
is really troubling you,

109
00:05:38,970 --> 00:05:40,830
there are things we could do about it.

110
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:42,360
For instance?

111
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:43,930
Well, the first thing that comes to mind

112
00:05:43,930 --> 00:05:45,310
is isolating the part of your brain

113
00:05:45,310 --> 00:05:48,470
where the memory is stored and destroying it with a laser.

114
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:51,690
Hmm, no.

115
00:05:51,690 --> 00:05:52,760
One slip of the hand,

116
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:54,890
and suddenly I'm sitting in the Engineering Department,

117
00:05:54,890 --> 00:05:57,500
building doodads with Wolowitz.

118
00:05:58,200 --> 00:05:59,610
All right.

119
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:02,020
Have you considered improving your socialization skills,

120
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:02,680
thus allowing you

121
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:04,880
to communicate more effectively with other people?

122
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:06,260
Isn't that their burden?

123
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:08,780
I'm the one with something interesting to say.

124
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:11,620
Fair enough, but in its essence,

125
00:06:11,620 --> 00:06:13,400
teaching is a performance art.

126
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:14,780
In the classroom paradigm,

127
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:17,130
the teacher has the responsibility to communicate,

128
00:06:17,130 --> 00:06:20,030
as well as entertain and engage.

129
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:22,630
I sense that you're trying to slow-walk me to an epiphany.

130
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:24,960
Would you mind very much jumping to it?

131
00:06:25,710 --> 00:06:28,590
Perhaps you should consider taking acting lessons.

132
00:06:28,590 --> 00:06:30,440
Acting lessons.

133
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:32,320
Interesting.

134
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:34,540
It might help if I could act as though I care

135
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:37,440
about my students and whether or not they learn.

136
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:41,650
Penny.

137
00:06:41,650 --> 00:06:44,380
Penny. Penny.

138
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:46,980
What?

139
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:49,790
You're an "actress," correct?

140
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,170
I'm not an "actress."

141
00:06:52,170 --> 00:06:53,990
I'm an actress.

142
00:06:55,380 --> 00:06:56,080
All right.

143
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:58,030
You're an actress.

144
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,100
I need you to teach me.

145
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:02,400
You want an acting lesson?

146
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:05,550
Perhaps two. I'd like to master the craft.

147
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:08,890
Okay, where is this coming from?

148
00:07:08,890 --> 00:07:12,050
It has been suggested to me that acting techniques

149
00:07:12,050 --> 00:07:14,170
could improve my lecturing, at which,

150
00:07:14,170 --> 00:07:16,530
if certain tweets and blogs are to be believed,

151
00:07:16,530 --> 00:07:18,030
I "suck the big one."

152
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,200
Yeah, I saw those. They were funny.

153
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:23,750
I printed a few out and put 'em on my fridge.

154
00:07:24,710 --> 00:07:26,280
So, when could we start?

155
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:27,680
Okay, just to be clear.

156
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:30,350
You are asking me for help because I know something

157
00:07:30,350 --> 00:07:33,440
that the brilliant Dr. Sheldon Cooper doesn't.

158
00:07:34,150 --> 00:07:36,740
I suppose that's one way to look at it.

159
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:39,840
I think it's the only way to look at it.

160
00:07:40,310 --> 00:07:41,590
Are you going to help me or not?

161
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:43,880
Probably. I'm just enjoying the foreplay.

162
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:47,770
Does this mean you are done mocking my acting career?

163
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:50,370
Oh, I'm sorry, I thought making the transition

164
00:07:50,370 --> 00:07:52,290
from actor to acting teacher

165
00:07:52,290 --> 00:07:53,710
was the signal that one's career

166
00:07:53,710 --> 00:07:55,950
had reached the end of the road.

167
00:07:57,820 --> 00:07:58,580
Forget it.

168
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:00,460
I'll pay you $40.

169
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,060
Saturday, 9:00 a.m. Bring cash.

170
00:08:05,790 --> 00:08:07,210
Raj, I have amazing news!

171
00:08:07,210 --> 00:08:08,010
What?

172
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:09,290
I just got offered a fellowship

173
00:08:09,290 --> 00:08:11,460
at the Weitzmann Institute in Israel.

174
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:13,300
- Dude, that's incredible!  - I know.

175
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:16,390
The only thing is, I'm gonna be gone for two years.

176
00:08:16,390 --> 00:08:18,190
Aw, I'm gonna miss you.

177
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:19,170
Are you going with him?

178
00:08:19,170 --> 00:08:20,780
I have to stay here for school.

179
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:22,920
That's what we're here to talk to you about.

180
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:26,350
You see, Bernadette has needs.

181
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:30,810
What kind of needs?

182
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:32,630
Sexual needs.

183
00:08:33,570 --> 00:08:34,820
Most of them regular,

184
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:37,270
some of them kind of messed up.

185
00:08:40,530 --> 00:08:41,990
So, while I'm gone,

186
00:08:41,990 --> 00:08:44,920
you're going to have to satisfy her.

187
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:47,570
What do you say?

188
00:08:48,590 --> 00:08:50,780
I say okey-dokey.

189
00:08:57,240 --> 00:08:59,660
What ya thinking so hard about?

190
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:02,590
Just that I'm definitely not gay.

191
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:11,590
Penny!

192
00:09:11,590 --> 00:09:13,790
Penny! Penny!

193
00:09:14,550 --> 00:09:15,380
What's wrong?

194
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:17,550
Nothing. I was acting.

195
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:20,710
You were acting.

196
00:09:20,710 --> 00:09:23,120
Yes. In preparation for today's studies,

197
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:25,620
I read Stanislavski's An Actor Prepares,

198
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:27,520
Stella Adler's The Technique of Acting,

199
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:29,270
Uta Hagen's Respect for Acting,

200
00:09:29,270 --> 00:09:32,460
and Henry Winkler's Ayyy, I'm an Actor.

201
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:34,480
Well, good for you.

202
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:35,820
Come on in.

203
00:09:36,410 --> 00:09:37,670
How shall we begin?

204
00:09:37,670 --> 00:09:39,060
Well, I thought we'd start

205
00:09:39,060 --> 00:09:41,580
with some basic movement exercises.

206
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:43,450
You know, get our bodies warmed up a little.

207
00:09:43,450 --> 00:09:44,310
All right.

208
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:45,600
So I just want you to relax

209
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,760
and kind of move around in the space.

210
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,830
You know, just do whatever feels natural.

211
00:09:58,350 --> 00:09:59,430
Sheldon?

212
00:09:59,430 --> 00:10:02,280
You said to do whatever feels natural.

213
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:04,140
This feels natural.

214
00:10:04,810 --> 00:10:05,600
Certainly more natural

215
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:07,010
than what you're doing.

216
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:08,770
Come on, you got to work with me.

217
00:10:08,770 --> 00:10:10,910
We need to get connected with our bodies.

218
00:10:10,910 --> 00:10:13,490
Penny, my body and I have a relationship

219
00:10:13,490 --> 00:10:15,700
that works best when we maintain a cool,

220
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:17,920
wary distance from each other.

221
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:23,140
All right, let's just say we've warmed up.

222
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:24,660
You're the "teacher" .

223
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:28,710
Okay. One of the things that might help you

224
00:10:28,710 --> 00:10:30,100
in connecting with your students

225
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:32,360
is being a little more spontaneous.

226
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:34,110
So why don't we try some improvisation?

227
00:10:34,110 --> 00:10:35,250
Why not? It seems like

228
00:10:35,250 --> 00:10:37,780
you're improvising your entire curriculum.

229
00:10:39,970 --> 00:10:42,700
This is all about listening and responding.

230
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:43,360
Gotcha.

231
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:44,470
I'm going to create a character

232
00:10:44,470 --> 00:10:46,580
and a situation, and you just jump in when you feel it.

233
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:48,060
- All right.  - All right.

234
00:10:48,270 --> 00:10:49,570
Action.

235
00:10:52,290 --> 00:10:53,820
Okay, it's not a movie.

236
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:55,910
It's improv. So no one calls "action" .

237
00:10:55,910 --> 00:10:58,010
Hey, you taught me something.

238
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:00,620
Who would have thought it?

239
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:04,260
Okay.

240
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:05,760
Uh, no, the shipment has not arrived,

241
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:06,950
and I really need those shoes.

242
00:11:06,950 --> 00:11:08,350
They are my biggest seller.

243
00:11:08,350 --> 00:11:10,510
Yes, ladies sizes six through ten.

244
00:11:10,510 --> 00:11:11,540
Thank you. Oh, sorry.

245
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:13,670
I have to go. I have a customer. Bye-bye.

246
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:15,810
Hi. Can I help you?

247
00:11:15,810 --> 00:11:18,490
I'd like a frozen yogurt, please.

248
00:11:22,370 --> 00:11:23,930
Yogurt?

249
00:11:24,570 --> 00:11:25,290
Okay, sure.

250
00:11:25,290 --> 00:11:29,220
Luckily, we sell both shoes and yogurt here.

251
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:32,180
You do?

252
00:11:33,450 --> 00:11:35,070
Yes. Look up at the sign,

253
00:11:35,070 --> 00:11:38,650
and remember, improv is always about saying yes.

254
00:11:38,650 --> 00:11:40,300
All right.

255
00:11:40,730 --> 00:11:42,480
Yes.

256
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:45,340
I see a sign.

257
00:11:45,340 --> 00:11:49,510
It says "Camarillo State Mental Hospital" .

258
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:52,220
What?

259
00:11:52,220 --> 00:11:54,340
It's the only explanation I can come up with

260
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:57,700
for why you think you sell shoes and yogurt.

261
00:11:58,700 --> 00:11:59,520
Okay, you know what?

262
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:01,930
Let's just try a different improv. Uh..

263
00:12:01,930 --> 00:12:04,200
Oh, this time we will be two winos

264
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:06,300
living under a freeway overpass.

265
00:12:06,300 --> 00:12:08,500
Oh, and we're going to use props?

266
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:10,040
You bet.

267
00:12:12,510 --> 00:12:14,060
I had dreams, you know.

268
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:15,550
I was gonna be famous.

269
00:12:15,550 --> 00:12:17,200
Show everybody back home I could be someone.

270
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:18,640
Now look at me.

271
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:21,580
Want some?

272
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:23,810
You have any frozen yogurt?

273
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:29,630
